  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 108 AM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nOn Saturday, upper trough deepens just to our west, as a mass of dry \nair moves into the mountains. The incoming trough will allow for \nsome synoptic forcing, meaning that convection won't turn off \ncompletely, but the influx of dry air will greatly limit the extent \nand strength of storms compared to the previous several days. Some \nconvection will still be present over the Continental Divide in the \nafternoon, while most of the plains remain dry. The exception to \nthis will be late Saturday afternoon into evening, as another cold \nfront moves in from the north, carrying enough low-level instability \nand moisture to allow for some brief showers and storms. These \nstorms will hit the Palmer First, spreading south and east into \nSaturday Night. High temperatures will be similar to those on \nFriday, though a few degrees cooler out east thanks to the front.\n\nSunday will see more dry air working into the mountains, reducing \npotential convection to near-zero. Meanwhile, lingering showers in \nthe morning will potentially limit instability for afternoon \nconvection out east. That being said, low-level moisture will hang \naround, with upsloping post-frontal winds over most of the eastern \nplains into the afternoon hours. Some isolated to scattered storms \nare expected to form over the Palmer Divide and, if they can last \nlong enough to move east, they will encounter better air and \nintensify. Looking at convective parameters, models do show CAPE \nabove 1000-1200 J/kg in the afternoon, with around 30-35 knots of \nshear depending on which model you go with. As such, any longer-\nlasting storms will have the potential to become stronger to low-end \nsevere, lasting into Sunday Night. Meanwhile, temperatures will \ncontinue their cooling trend, with 70s to low-80s over most of our \nforecast area.\n\nConfidence in the model solutions drops quickly as we look into next \nweek. The general consensus is for a broad upper low to move onto \nthe CA coast, pushing east towards midweek. This will bring more \nPacific moisture back to our region, along with generally cooler \ntemperatures. Other than better chances for more widespread \nprecipitation, we will have to wait until models are in better \nagreement with the evolution of the system for more specific \nimpacts.",
